{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition is the main extension mechanism in TanStack Charts. Marks share a\nchart specification, scales, theme, and responsive plot rectangle. More\ncomplex views combine those same primitives instead of switching to a separate\ncomponent family."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"layer-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks are rendered in array order. Put broad background geometry first and\nannotations or direct labels last:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleY([0]),\n    areaY(rows, { x: 'date', y1: 'low', y2: 'high' }),\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'median', text: 'label' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mark may consume a different datum type. The resulting chart interaction\ntype is the honest union of those datum types. Use ordinary TypeScript\nnarrowing when a callback handles several layers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The canonical grammar is described in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/marks-and-layering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks and Layering"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"facet-one-view-by-a-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facet one view by a field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetChart"},{"type":"text","value":" repeats a mark composition for each group and returns a complete\nguide-free outer definition:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"regional-facets-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"regional-facets","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { dot, facetChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nexport default facetChart(rows, {\\n  by: 'region',\\n  columns: 3,\\n  gap: 16,\\n  axes: 'outer',\\n  label: (region) => String(region),\\n  chart(data) {\\n    return {\\n      marks: [\\n        lineY(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', strokeWidth: 2 }),\\n        dot(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', r: 3.5 }),\\n      ],\\n      scales: {\\n        x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 4]) },\\n        y: {\\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 80]),\\n          grid: true,\\n          axis: { label: 'Orders' },\\n        },\\n      },\\n    }\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const rows = [\\n  { region: 'North', week: 1, orders: 32 },\\n  { region: 'North', week: 2, orders: 46 },\\n  { region: 'North', week: 3, orders: 51 },\\n  { region: 'North', week: 4, orders: 64 },\\n  { region: 'South', week: 1, orders: 45 },\\n  { region: 'South', week: 2, orders: 42 },\\n  { region: 'South', week: 3, orders: 57 },\\n  { region: 'South', week: 4, orders: 61 },\\n  { region: 'West', week: 1, orders: 25 },\\n  { region: 'West', week: 2, orders: 39 },\\n  { region: 'West', week: 3, orders: 48 },\\n  { region: 'West', week: 4, orders: 70 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { dot, facetChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default facetChart(rows, {\n  by: 'region',\n  columns: 3,\n  gap: 16,\n  axes: 'outer',\n  label: (region) => String(region),\n  chart(data) {\n    return {\n      marks: [\n        lineY(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', strokeWidth: 2 }),\n        dot(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', r: 3.5 }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 4]) },\n        y: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 80]),\n          grid: true,\n          axis: { label: 'Orders' },\n        },\n      },\n    }\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=regional-facets env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const rows = [\n  { region: 'North', week: 1, orders: 32 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 2, orders: 46 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 3, orders: 51 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 4, orders: 64 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 1, orders: 45 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 2, orders: 42 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 3, orders: 57 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 4, orders: 61 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 1, orders: 25 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 2, orders: 39 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 3, orders: 48 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 4, orders: 70 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=regional-facets file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facet(rows, options)"},{"type":"text","value":" instead when the repeated panels need to be one mark\ninside a larger custom definition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axes: 'outer'"},{"type":"text","value":" draws shared guides around the complete facet\ngrid. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axes: 'cell'"},{"type":"text","value":" when each panel needs its own guides. Cell axes and\nincompatible independent scales cannot be presented as one shared outer axis;\nchoose the option that matches the comparison."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/facets-anscombe/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the Anscombe quartet catalog case"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"share-domains-intentionally","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Share domains intentionally"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared scales make position comparable across panels. Independent domains\nmake local variation easier to see but can exaggerate differences."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build shared domains once and pass configured scales to every facet. When a\npanel deliberately uses an independent domain, label that policy in the\nsurrounding UI."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" page owns scale construction\nand responsive range rules."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compose-distinct-views","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compose distinct views"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not every composition is a facet. A focus-and-context chart, scatterplot with\nmarginal histograms, or chart with an inset summary contains views with\ndifferent roles. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"composeViews"},{"type":"text","value":" when they belong to one accessible figure:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { alignX, composeViews, grid } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    overview: overviewDefinition,\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: grid({\n    rows: [\n      { id: 'overview', size: 72 },\n      { id: 'detail', grow: 1 },\n    ],\n    columns: [{ id: 'main', grow: 1 }],\n    cells: {\n      overview: { row: 'overview', column: 'main' },\n      detail: { row: 'detail', column: 'main' },\n    },\n  }),\n  links: [alignX('overview', 'detail')],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layer"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":" when views overlap. Later layers paint above\nearlier layers, and every child is clipped to its resolved frame. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shareX"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shareY"},{"type":"text","value":" when linked views must resolve the same scale domain and\nmapping; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alignX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alignY"},{"type":"text","value":" align plot endpoints without sharing domains."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewGrid"},{"type":"text","value":" is convenience syntax for the non-overlapping grid case. Keep\nsemantic selection or viewport state in application state; each child\ndefinition consumes that state as ordinary data or domains. Use separate chart\nhosts when panels need independent tooltips, keyboard behavior, or accessible\nlabels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not coordinate charts by querying or mutating their SVG nodes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"composition-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer order reflects visual occlusion and reading order."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mark keeps its natural data shape and stable inferred or explicit\nidentity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared scales are used only where direct positional comparison is intended."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facet axis policy is explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"composeViews"},{"type":"text","value":" child is placed exactly once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A composed definition has one outer accessible chart label and host behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independently hosted views have independent accessible labels."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared state is semantic application state, not DOM state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dense dashboards destroy hosts and listeners when panels unmount."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"layer-marks","text":"Layer marks","level":2},{"id":"facet-one-view-by-a-field","text":"Facet one view by a field","level":2},{"id":"share-domains-intentionally","text":"Share domains intentionally","level":2},{"id":"compose-distinct-views","text":"Compose distinct views","level":2},{"id":"composition-checklist","text":"Composition checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nComposition is the main extension mechanism in TanStack Charts. Marks share a\nchart specification, scales, theme, and responsive plot rectangle. More\ncomplex views combine those same primitives instead of switching to a separate\ncomponent family.\n\n## Layer marks\n\nMarks are rendered in array order. Put broad background geometry first and\nannotations or direct labels last:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleY([0]),\n    areaY(rows, { x: 'date', y1: 'low', y2: 'high' }),\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'median', text: 'label' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nEach mark may consume a different datum type. The resulting chart interaction\ntype is the honest union of those datum types. Use ordinary TypeScript\nnarrowing when a callback handles several layers.\n\nThe canonical grammar is described in\n[Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md).\n\n## Facet one view by a field\n\n`facetChart` repeats a mark composition for each group and returns a complete\nguide-free outer definition:\n\n```ts group=regional-facets env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { dot, facetChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default facetChart(rows, {\n  by: 'region',\n  columns: 3,\n  gap: 16,\n  axes: 'outer',\n  label: (region) => String(region),\n  chart(data) {\n    return {\n      marks: [\n        lineY(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', strokeWidth: 2 }),\n        dot(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', r: 3.5 }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 4]) },\n        y: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 80]),\n          grid: true,\n          axis: { label: 'Orders' },\n        },\n      },\n    }\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=regional-facets file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { region: 'North', week: 1, orders: 32 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 2, orders: 46 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 3, orders: 51 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 4, orders: 64 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 1, orders: 45 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 2, orders: 42 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 3, orders: 57 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 4, orders: 61 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 1, orders: 25 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 2, orders: 39 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 3, orders: 48 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 4, orders: 70 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `facet(rows, options)` instead when the repeated panels need to be one mark\ninside a larger custom definition.\n\nThe default `axes: 'outer'` draws shared guides around the complete facet\ngrid. Use `axes: 'cell'` when each panel needs its own guides. Cell axes and\nincompatible independent scales cannot be presented as one shared outer axis;\nchoose the option that matches the comparison.\n\n[Open the Anscombe quartet catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/facets-anscombe/).\n\n## Share domains intentionally\n\nShared scales make position comparable across panels. Independent domains\nmake local variation easier to see but can exaggerate differences.\n\nBuild shared domains once and pass configured scales to every facet. When a\npanel deliberately uses an independent domain, label that policy in the\nsurrounding UI.\n\nThe [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) page owns scale construction\nand responsive range rules.\n\n## Compose distinct views\n\nNot every composition is a facet. A focus-and-context chart, scatterplot with\nmarginal histograms, or chart with an inset summary contains views with\ndifferent roles. Use `composeViews` when they belong to one accessible figure:\n\n```ts\nimport { alignX, composeViews, grid } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    overview: overviewDefinition,\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: grid({\n    rows: [\n      { id: 'overview', size: 72 },\n      { id: 'detail', grow: 1 },\n    ],\n    columns: [{ id: 'main', grow: 1 }],\n    cells: {\n      overview: { row: 'overview', column: 'main' },\n      detail: { row: 'detail', column: 'main' },\n    },\n  }),\n  links: [alignX('overview', 'detail')],\n})\n```\n\nUse `fill`, `layer`, and `inset` when views overlap. Later layers paint above\nearlier layers, and every child is clipped to its resolved frame. Use\n`shareX` or `shareY` when linked views must resolve the same scale domain and\nmapping; `alignX` and `alignY` align plot endpoints without sharing domains.\n\n`viewGrid` is convenience syntax for the non-overlapping grid case. Keep\nsemantic selection or viewport state in application state; each child\ndefinition consumes that state as ordinary data or domains. Use separate chart\nhosts when panels need independent tooltips, keyboard behavior, or accessible\nlabels.\n\nDo not coordinate charts by querying or mutating their SVG nodes.\n\n## Composition checklist\n\n- Layer order reflects visual occlusion and reading order.\n- Each mark keeps its natural data shape and stable inferred or explicit\n  identity.\n- Shared scales are used only where direct positional comparison is intended.\n- Facet axis policy is explicit.\n- Every named `composeViews` child is placed exactly once.\n- A composed definition has one outer accessible chart label and host behavior.\n- Independently hosted views have independent accessible labels.\n- Shared state is semantic application state, not DOM state.\n- Dense dashboards destroy hosts and listeners when panels unmount.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Faceting and Composition\ndescription: Layer marks, repeat a shared chart by group, and coordinate multiple views without introducing a fixed chart-type model.\n---\n\nComposition is the main extension mechanism in TanStack Charts. Marks share a\nchart specification, scales, theme, and responsive plot rectangle. More\ncomplex views combine those same primitives instead of switching to a separate\ncomponent family.\n\n## Layer marks\n\nMarks are rendered in array order. Put broad background geometry first and\nannotations or direct labels last:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleY([0]),\n    areaY(rows, { x: 'date', y1: 'low', y2: 'high' }),\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'median' }),\n    text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'median', text: 'label' }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nEach mark may consume a different datum type. The resulting chart interaction\ntype is the honest union of those datum types. Use ordinary TypeScript\nnarrowing when a callback handles several layers.\n\nThe canonical grammar is described in\n[Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md).\n\n## Facet one view by a field\n\n`facetChart` repeats a mark composition for each group and returns a complete\nguide-free outer definition:\n\n```ts group=regional-facets env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { dot, facetChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default facetChart(rows, {\n  by: 'region',\n  columns: 3,\n  gap: 16,\n  axes: 'outer',\n  label: (region) => String(region),\n  chart(data) {\n    return {\n      marks: [\n        lineY(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', strokeWidth: 2 }),\n        dot(data, { x: 'week', y: 'orders', r: 3.5 }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 4]) },\n        y: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 80]),\n          grid: true,\n          axis: { label: 'Orders' },\n        },\n      },\n    }\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=regional-facets file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { region: 'North', week: 1, orders: 32 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 2, orders: 46 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 3, orders: 51 },\n  { region: 'North', week: 4, orders: 64 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 1, orders: 45 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 2, orders: 42 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 3, orders: 57 },\n  { region: 'South', week: 4, orders: 61 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 1, orders: 25 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 2, orders: 39 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 3, orders: 48 },\n  { region: 'West', week: 4, orders: 70 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `facet(rows, options)` instead when the repeated panels need to be one mark\ninside a larger custom definition.\n\nThe default `axes: 'outer'` draws shared guides around the complete facet\ngrid. Use `axes: 'cell'` when each panel needs its own guides. Cell axes and\nincompatible independent scales cannot be presented as one shared outer axis;\nchoose the option that matches the comparison.\n\n[Open the Anscombe quartet catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/facets-anscombe/).\n\n## Share domains intentionally\n\nShared scales make position comparable across panels. Independent domains\nmake local variation easier to see but can exaggerate differences.\n\nBuild shared domains once and pass configured scales to every facet. When a\npanel deliberately uses an independent domain, label that policy in the\nsurrounding UI.\n\nThe [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) page owns scale construction\nand responsive range rules.\n\n## Compose distinct views\n\nNot every composition is a facet. A focus-and-context chart, scatterplot with\nmarginal histograms, or chart with an inset summary contains views with\ndifferent roles. Use `composeViews` when they belong to one accessible figure:\n\n```ts\nimport { alignX, composeViews, grid } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    overview: overviewDefinition,\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: grid({\n    rows: [\n      { id: 'overview', size: 72 },\n      { id: 'detail', grow: 1 },\n    ],\n    columns: [{ id: 'main', grow: 1 }],\n    cells: {\n      overview: { row: 'overview', column: 'main' },\n      detail: { row: 'detail', column: 'main' },\n    },\n  }),\n  links: [alignX('overview', 'detail')],\n})\n```\n\nUse `fill`, `layer`, and `inset` when views overlap. Later layers paint above\nearlier layers, and every child is clipped to its resolved frame. Use\n`shareX` or `shareY` when linked views must resolve the same scale domain and\nmapping; `alignX` and `alignY` align plot endpoints without sharing domains.\n\n`viewGrid` is convenience syntax for the non-overlapping grid case. Keep\nsemantic selection or viewport state in application state; each child\ndefinition consumes that state as ordinary data or domains. Use separate chart\nhosts when panels need independent tooltips, keyboard behavior, or accessible\nlabels.\n\nDo not coordinate charts by querying or mutating their SVG nodes.\n\n## Composition checklist\n\n- Layer order reflects visual occlusion and reading order.\n- Each mark keeps its natural data shape and stable inferred or explicit\n  identity.\n- Shared scales are used only where direct positional comparison is intended.\n- Facet axis policy is explicit.\n- Every named `composeViews` child is placed exactly once.\n- A composed definition has one outer accessible chart label and host behavior.\n- Independently hosted views have independent accessible labels.\n- Shared state is semantic application state, not DOM state.\n- Dense dashboards destroy hosts and listeners when panels unmount.\n"}
